\chapter{Constructing Creoles, Cajuns, and F\&C}
  \label{chap:discourse}
  The social categories of Creole and Cajun and their language are not static.
  There are general tendencies for which subject pronouns Creoles and Cajuns use when speaking F\&C, and their personal networks are suggestive of a social structure where they are distant from each other, but they can also construct their individual ethnic identities relative to these more general patterns.
  In turn, these constructions feed back into ideologies surrounding F\&C.
  The objective of this chapter, then, is to examine the discourse of Creoles and Cajuns, with a special attention to subject pronouns, in order to better understand how these processes play out in the community.

  \section{Identity construction in sociolinguistics}
    \label{sec:racial_discourse_review}
    % Yet another new outline -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
