    What it means for discourse to be racializing may not be immediately clear.
    Racializing discourse has generally been defined as that which ``sorts some people, things, places, and practices into social categories marked as inherently dangerous and Other'' \parencite[p.~E2]{dick_racializing_2011}.
    Often used as a synonym for racializing discourse is the term \lexi{race talk}, which shares with the above definition the idea that such talk always aims to degrade \parencite{morrison_backs_1993}.
    However, affirming or reaffirming racialized social categories does not necessarily have this goal or impact.
    For example, when Black activists in Oklahoma highlighted the role of race in how their neighborhood was being neglected, they were necessarily engaging in racializing discourse, but it was being put into action to counteract the colorblind racism \parencite{bonilla-silva_racism_2022} of the larger community \parencite[p.~E79]{blanton_chronotopic_2011}.
    Indeed, it has been suggested that marginalized racialized groups should variably choose to discursively transgress against racial logics when they are not beneficial to the group and reaffirm them when needed to draw attention to inequalities born of their racialization \parencite{alim_whos_2016}.
    Naturally, this also means that those who produce or reproduce racial orders through discourse are not necessarily ideologically racist \parencite{hodges_ideologies_2015}.
    When racializing discourse is the expression of ideological racism, latent or not, it is often covert so as to provide plausible deniability in order to avoid possible societal sanctions \parencite[p.~E2]{dick_racializing_2011}.
    The working definition of racializing discourse for the present study is thus any discourse that signals race in some way, overtly or covertly, with positive or with negative intentions or effects.
    In short, it is discourse that produces or reproduces the process of racialization.

    One framework for racializing discourse is the concept of boundaries \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},]{myers_race_2001}.\footnote{
      While boundary concepts such as social closure come from the Weberian tradition rather than the Marxist tradition associated with racialization, this does not render boundaries off-limits here.
      Boundaries between racialized groups plainly exist, as Chapter \ref{chap:networks} demonstrated, and as this chapter will show, those boundaries can be enforced by individual actors.
    }
    Establishing boundaries effectively establishes racialized groups and characterizes them.
    Those boundaries can then be policed or maintained, or in other words, actors can deny others access into their group's boundaries or prevent in-group members from crossing another groups boundaries, respectively.
    \citeauthor{myers_race_2001} (\citeyear{myers_race_2001}) work is particularly relevant for the current study as they focused on racializing discourses in private conversations which more or less describes the sort of discourse that was collected here.
    They found that boundaries were marked (\foreign{i.e.}, categories were created and characterized) for marginalized groups through racial slurs, caricatures of others, and the dehumanization of anything associated with those groups.
    White people then policed those boundaries by rejecting the right of Black people to exist in White spaces, whereas Black people maintained boundaries instead (\foreign{i.e.}, self-policed).
    The fact that the mechanisms available to the two groups to protect boundaries were indicative of their differing positions in a social hierarchy sets these sort of boundaries apart from those which may be established between groups in non-racializing situations where the marginalization of a people is not inherent to the social structure.

    One group that can undoubtedly be qualified as racialized are Black people in the United States, which has led to English discourse analysis around this group in terms of race and racialization.
    The Creoles of interest for this study being located in the same country as well as the fractal recursivity \parencite{irvine_language_2000} that has equated Creoles with Black people makes the latter a highly relevant comparison group.
    It is thus informative to examine how racialized boundaries are marked for Black people and White people, how White people police these boundaries, and how Black people maintain them.

    % \subsection{Boundary marking}
    Racialized boundaries in the US have been marked out in many ways, naming groups being perhaps the most straightforward way, but another plain way is through the creation of physical boundaries that have persisted since the time of \lexi{de jure} segregation.
    As a result, the theme of neglected, crime-ridden neighborhoods being associated with Black Americans is consistent in discourse not just among White Americans but also among Black Americans who have direct experience with such neighborhoods (\foreign{e.g.}, \citeauthor{becker_linguistic_2014}, \citeyear[p.~46]{becker_linguistic_2014}; \citeauthor{rickford_addressee-_1994}, \citeyear[p.~243]{rickford_addressee-_1994}).
    Additionally, this regular juxtaposition of Black Americans with run-down geographic locations functions as a way of characterizing the people themselves \parencite[p.~E81]{blanton_chronotopic_2011}.
    % Boundaries can coincide with literal physical boundaries that are clear enough for those in the community to comment on and fight over (Blanton 2011)
      % "[B]lack folk" are collocated both in space and discourse with polluted waterways not even fit for horses (Blanton 2011:E81)

    More subtly, pronouns such as \lexi{us} and \lexi{them} can be used to establish that two groups exist that are socially, and in this case racially, differentiated \parencite[p.~E80]{blanton_chronotopic_2011}.
    This is often done by more privileged groups to avoid possibly implicating oneself as racist.
    For example, as one Lumbee Indian explained, ``[T]hey [American Indians] didn't fool with them [Black Americans], they were scared of them'' \parencite[pp.~178-179]{schilling-estes_constructing_2004}.
    The speaker was himself an American Indian, providing the implicature that these were also his own feelings, but he was able to avoid acknowledging his own racial ideology by speaking through his group instead, allowing for plausible deniability if confronted about the statement.
    A \lexi{they} may also be used by marginalized groups in ways to emphasize their own discrimination and background the importance of who the exact discriminators are \parencite[p.~1020]{perkins_discursive_2019}.
    % For those at the top of the hierarchy, this can be done when beneficial to their discourse whereas the us-them dynamic can be disregarded when not beneficial (Blanton 2011:E83/E86)

    These processes do not stop at defining boundaries and naming those on the other side of them but also extend to characterizing what makes each group different.
    In a racialized social system, the marginalized group is thus given negative associations which may be as direct as dehumanizing such people as ``trash'' \parencite[p.~329]{okeke_race_2025}, but typically in the US today, marginalized groups are characterized more covertly as in the above collations between Black Americans and neglected neighborhoods.
    This can manifest in a Black woman being described as ``irritated,'' having ``absolutely no respect,'' and being ``dumber than a box of rocks'' after giving a court testimony \parencite[p.~761]{baugh_swb_2015} to being described as physically imposing to the point of being ``scary'' \parencite[p.~453]{bucholtz_you_1999}, both of which were the sort of characterizations that Black undergraduate students have also reported \parencite[pp.~425-427]{holliday_sociolinguistic_2021}.
    What these characterizations have in common is that they aim to focus on the physicality of Black people and delegitimize their intelligence, rendering them more like strong animals than thoughtful humans.
    This need not even be with the conscious goal of harming these people as, indeed, the individual who used the ``scary'' descriptor exploited African American Language to positively index his own strength, but the impact is the same nonetheless.

    Negative characterizations of marginalized racialized groups are not limited to higher status groups, however, as those who are marginalized may reproduce stereotypes about themselves.
    One example of this is Black Americans judging their own responsibility with money in a negative light \parencite[p.~1018]{perkins_discursive_2019}.
    This particular case involved explicitly contrasting the spending habits of Black Americans with those of a wealthy White man.
    Racializing discourse can become so ingrained in a society that even those who suffer from the resulting social structure may find themselves perpetuating it.
    % And then they may downplay the importance of the discrimination they face as simply how the world is, not even distinguishing their own experience from that of other minority groups (K. Perkins et al. 2019:1020-1021)
      % This is essentially a coping mechanism

    % \subsection{Boundary policing}
    Once boundaries have been marked out, they need to be enforced.
    For the privileged group in the racial hierarchy, in this case White Americans, this can consist of making marginalized people unwelcome in public spaces or unwelcome to resources.
    One expression of this is Black people not being able to find institutional redress when falsely accused of wrong-doing \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][p.~3]{labov_facto_1986}.
    Likewise, when attempting to bring to light the wrongs that White Americans have done to them, Black Americans may find themselves facing sanctions rather than the wrong-doers \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][pp.~281-282]{morgan_more_2022}.
    In essence, the sort of public institutions that would deal with these issues are seen as the domains of White Americans.

    Another way to deny Black Americans access to what are treated as White spaces is to place on them the burden of learning new ways of speaking in academic and business contexts where a failure to do so can inhibit one's life chances \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][p.~367]{williams_looking_2010}.
    This burden has been argued to be a reproduction of the Jim Crow logic of separate but equal in that the language of Black 
    Americans is allowed to exist but must not do so in specific spaces \parencite{young_nah_2009}.
    \textcite{holliday_sociolinguistic_2021} called the ``physical, emotional, and psychological effort'' involved in balancing language varieties in this way sociolinguistic labor (p.~421).
    What this might look like in discourse is White Americans looking down on those who do not learn the correct way of speaking and Black Americans engaging in code-switching.

    This \foreign{status quo} of public spaces and resources being treated as the domain (\foreign{i.e.}, boundaries) of White Americans can ironically be maintained by denying the existence of race when shown to be present in uncomfortable places.
    Denial can be found in euphemisms that allow White Americans to not have to directly deal with race by avoiding explicitly racial terms with those such as \lexi{ethnicity}, \lexi{diversity}, and \lexi{multiculturalism} \parencite[p.~E4]{dick_racializing_2011}.
    Likewise, racialized orders and their role in environmental injustice \parencite{blanton_chronotopic_2011} or the closing of Black schools \parencite[pp.~754-756]{briscoe_that_2015} is disregarded, making it impossible to address these problems.
    Not having to take race into account is a privilege afforded to White Americans and not Black Americans who must always be vigilant even when forming friendships \parencite[p.~11]{wilson_wait_2025}.
    This is further facilitated by folk theories of race that only allow personal, ideological racism to exist and not systemic racism \parencite{hodges_ideologies_2015}.
    Marginalized people are not entirely unaware of this colorblind strategy and so may take terms meant to politely apply to them as euphemisms for the plainly derogatory terms instead \parencite[pp.~E80-E81]{blanton_chronotopic_2011}.
    However, attempting to bring up the racial underpinnings of such discourse can be met with marginalized people being paradoxically accused of being racist themselves (\citeauthor{blanton_chronotopic_2011}, \citeyear[pp.~E83-E84/E88]{blanton_chronotopic_2011}; \citeauthor{briscoe_that_2015}, \citeyear[p.~756]{briscoe_that_2015}).
    Racializing discourse along these lines renders racialized social systems particularly intransigent.
      
    % \subsection{Boundary maintenance}
    White Americans have access not only to the mechanism of boundary policing, denying others access to their spaces and resources, but also boundary maintenance, in which one sanctions other in-group members for not protecting the group's social space.
    By contrast, Black Americans may police boundaries in minor ways such as criticizing White Americans for tanning themselves to look more Black \parencite[pp.~15-16]{myers_race_2001}, but primarily Black Americans only have access to the latter mechanism because the racialized social system has already excluded them from having the power to control public spaces.
    One form that boundary maintaining might take for White Americans is through parenting in which they, for example, allow their children to have Black friends at school but not to go to their homes or have romantic relationships with them \parencite{carter_racialization_2012}.
    Another form of boundary maintenance sees Black Americans criticizing other Black Americans for academic achievements under the belief that academia is a White space \parencite[p.~16]{brown_useful_2012}.\footnote{
      The oppositional culture theory this comes from has been generally rejected as an explanation for African American educational achievement \parencite[p.~16]{brown_useful_2012}, but this does not mean that such discourse does not occur.
    } For a Black American to advance in their education is thus to abandon their peers and embrace the culture of their oppressors.
    % Relatedly, Whites who moved to predominantly Black schools in their early years have reported being readily accepted, whereas Blacks who moved to predominantly White schools were ostracized (S. Carter et al. 2012)
      % Essentially, there was no reason to ostracize the White students in the Black schools because there is not a sense built into society that school is the domain of Blacks, whereas Whites may feel like their space is being invaded when Black students arrive

    % Racialized people are uneasy with labeling their identities
      % Becker's (2014) participant did not want to use the word African American because it was reductive (i.e., African is a large continent with many cultures) (47)

    Boundary marking, policing, and maintenance take on certain characteristics when used to produce and reproduce racialization in discourse.
    Once groups are identified, they are then characterized in ways to construct a hierarchy of humanization.
    This hierarchy is then defended on both sides, although the means of doing so is different for the groups involved.
    Those at the top of the hierarchy have the power to police boundaries, whereas the marginalized can only effectively carry out boundary maintenance.
    The following research question for this chapter therefore aims to search for signs of this same logic in the discourse of Creoles and Cajuns.

    \begin{itemize}
      \item[RQ3:] Is the discourse of Creoles and Cajuns in Louisiana indicative of the racialization of these groups in that it creates, polices, and maintains inequal boundaries?
    \end{itemize}

    \subsection{Racializing discourse in Louisiana}
      While this question has not been taken up before in the literature on Creoles and Cajuns, \citeauthor{wendte_tale_2020}'s (\citeyear{wendte_tale_2020}) work contains enlightening discourse data that can be re-examined from the angle of racialization.
      Wendte himself had the goal of qualitatively looking into how Texan and Louisianian Creoles who speak F\&C define what being Creole means to them.
      Importantly, he did not include Cajuns as primary participants, though he did report on some who insisted on being interviewed anyway.
      This limited the ability to speak about outgroup characterizations of Creoles.

      % Boundary marking
      Boundary marking can be found throughout \citeauthor{wendte_tale_2020}'s (\citeyear{wendte_tale_2020}) data.
      This took various forms such as associating different yet related cultural practices to each group -- zydeco versus Cajun music (pp.~139/207-208/211), spice levels in cooking (pp.~143/215) -- often exaggerating the differences in these practices, as well as contesting the meaning and associations surround terms taken pejoratively at times (pp.~190-191).
      As in the data for the present study, it was also clear that fractal recursivity played a role in both marking boundaries and characterizing those on either side of those boundaries (pp.~168/179/181-184/188-191/200-201/218-219/275-279/284-275/297-298).
      Fractal recursivity involves transferring a distinction at one level of social structure to another level \parencite[p.~38]{irvine_language_2000}, in this case transferring the non-White and White distinction of the broader US to the categories of Creole and Cajun in Louisiana, respectively.

      % "Others" are not good
        % Others can be a specific form of oneself
          % A woman from rural Evangeline Parish, she was herself Creole but a Creole who was ``plus cadien'' \gloss{more Cajun} (p.~275).
            % She appeared to mean that she was specifically not Black, describing herself instead as a mix of Spanish, European, and Native America, whereas others who call themselves Creoles were additionally Black (pp.~280-281).
            % In each of these groups, she rather bluntly explained that it was not good to mix with Blacks and expressed consternation at sometimes being labeled Black on official documents.
            % These statements implicitly acknowledged the widespread idea that Cajun is White and Creole is Black as this woman struggled to shed herself of the stigma of being considered Black and align herself instead with Whites to such an extent that she seemed to harbor a racist ideology even against herself as, during these exchanges, she presented Wendte with a DNA test she had done that showed that she had African ancestors.

      % Boundary maintenance
      Boundary maintenance was carried out primarily in two ways in \citeauthor{wendte_tale_2020}'s (\citeyear{wendte_tale_2020}) data.
      One was to downplay some of the above boundary marking mechanisms and instead associate being Creole with simply being a good and resourceful person (pp.~136/205).
      These positive traits appeared to be meant to entice would-be Creoles to embrace the category.
      Additionally, Creoles sometimes spoke about the need to remain closely associated with Louisiana and with F\&C.
      Those who migrated to Texas put at risk their ability to identify as Creole as did those who no longer engaged in cultural practices associated with Louisiana or who lost their ability to speak F\&C either completely or not well enough (pp.~133-135/137/146-147/153-155/202-203/224-225/227-228/230-232/289).

        % Don't move away from LA or abandon the language or you may no longer be Creole

      % Boundary policing
      One way in which boundary policing can take place in a racialized social system is by placing the burden of learning a standard language variety on those who have been marginalized.
      This is complicated when considering F\&C as this is now something of a heritage language, but \citeauthor{wendte_tale_2020}'s (\citeyear{wendte_tale_2020}) data did still demonstrate similar language ideology at play, expressed in terms of the belief that Cajuns speak F\&C better than Creoles (p.~176).
      This sentiment was even repeated by a Creole women who herself used all Cajun-marked subject pronouns yet believed that she and other Black people spoke worse than Cajuns (p.~255).

      Clearer examples of boundary policing came under the form of differing levels of restrictions on who could claim Cajun identity versus who could claim Creole identity.
      The former plainly required one to be able to say that they are also White (pp.~296-297).
      The complete absence of anyone attempting to override this requirement among the Louisiana participants suggests that this restriction is thoroughly enforced.
      On the other hand, Creole identity was open to almost anyone even though it was generally those who could not claim a White identity who identified as Creole.
      A striking example of this came when two participants self-identified as White Cajuns except in the presence of a Black friend, at which point they self-identified as Creoles.
      When this Black friend was in their company, she looked to them for permission to claim Creole as her identity when asked and denied altogether that she could claim a Cajun identity, too, like her friends did (pp.~293-294).
      In this case, Cajuns were not only policing the boundaries of Cajunness but also the boundaries of Creoleness.

      % Cajuns can even police who is allowed to be Creole
        % Finally, \citeauthor{wendte_tale_2020}'s (\citeyear{wendte_tale_2020}) Louisiana data suggested that there is a restriction to who can self-identify as a Cajun, higher in the racial hierarchy, that does not exist for identifying as a Creole.
        % The restriction for Cajun self-identification that was expressed was that one must be White to be Cajun (pp.~296-297).
        % A few Louisianian Creoles did initially try to say that Cajuns can be various colors but then always went on to imply that Cajuns were White or close to White (pp.~295-296).
        % For Creoles, however, the barrier to entry was signficantly more flexible.
        % At one point, a participant told Wendte that he himself could ``qualifier pour un Créole'' \gloss{qualify as a Creole} despite Wendte being a White non-Louisianian and non-native speaker of F\&C (p.~235).
        % The openness of the category was particularly true for those who understood it in the original sense as being no more than native to Louisiana.
        % Three participants from Ville Platte in Evangeline Parish had recently re-identified as Creole after learning this, whereas they spent their lives identifying as Cajun to avoid being considered Black if they had tried to identify as Creole (pp.~282-283).
        % This freedom then, was seen as effectively given to Whites but not non-Whites, and it was a freedom that could be fluid.
        % Two other participants from rural Evangeline Parish were reported identifying as White Cajuns except in the presence of a Black friend, at which point they identified as Creoles.
        % Additionally, this Black friend from Saint Landry Parish, while in their company, defined Cajuns as Frenchmen instead of White then claimed to not know her own identity when asked.
        % Instead, she looked to the other two for permission to identify as Creole and denied that she could be Cajun, as well, like her friends (pp.~293-294).
        % This is reminiscent of the communicative rules of \foreign{de jure} segregation that \textcite{morgan_more_2022} discussed where permission to speak is required for racialized people and where they were expected to say only what those of higher racial status wanted them to say, in this case even to the extent where the individual in the group who much more clearly could lay claim to being Creole did not feel comfortable doing so in front of Whites who were allowed to freely claim whatever identity they wanted.

      \citeauthor{wendte_tale_2020}'s (\citeyear{wendte_tale_2020}) work, despite not attempting to address race, still suggested that Creoles and Cajuns mark, police, and maintain boundaries for these two categories in their discourse much as Americans do for non-White and White categories broadly in the US.
      It was thus expected that the discourse data from the present study would be even more elucidating into how this is carried out given the addition of Cajun voices as well as focusing conversations on race when possible.

  \section{Analysis}
    \label{sec:discourse_analysis}
    Three aspects of the discourse of Creoles in Cajuns in F\&C will be covered here that will shed light on how they construct their identities and the role of language ideology in that venture.
    The first will focus on their more direct discussions of what Creoles and Cajuns are.
    It will be shown that, as the literature on ethnicity in Louisiana as well as comments from the general population have suggested, Whiteness and Blackness are emphasized in participants' definitions.
    The second will focus on meta-linguistic commentary surrounding F\&C, showing that Creoles and Cajuns differ in their estimations of just how differently Creoles and Cajuns speak.
    The third will focus on instances where speakers exploited the subject pronoun resources at their disposal for their indexical values or to take stances on the topic at hand.
    The overall picture that will emerge is that of two groups that hold different social positions are reiterated through their use of discourse.

    \subsection{Whiteness and Blackness}
      The purpose of examining the way Creoles and Cajuns define themselves and each other in more explicit terms is to set a sort of baseline for the image that these participants wish to consciously project.
      These more direct definitions provided by participants support the idea that the community associates Creoles with Blackness and Cajuns with Whiteness.
      However, this is association is made explicitly for Creoles more consistently than it is for Cajuns.
      This speaks to a social hierarchy that constructs Cajuns as privileged relative to Creoles who are marginalized.
      
      The Whiteness of Cajuns was generally filtered through discussions of ancestry, although Creoles were more likely to draw this connection in plain terms.
      In line with the Cajun origin story presented in Section \ref{sec:cajun_history}, Cajuns were seen as the descendants of the Acadians who were expelled from the Maritime provinces of Canada.
      Brian's reiteration of this idea in Sentence \ref{sent:cajun_brian} was a typical initial response when participants were asked to define \lexi{Cajun}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Brian\footnote{
                All participant names as well as all mentioned names have been anonymized.
              } (Cajun): Moi, je dis un Cadien, c'est du monde qui a venu de Canada.\footnote{
                Vocal noises, hesitations, and repetitions are not reproduced in quotes from participants except when they provide meaningful nuance to a participant's statement.
              } (48:14)\label{sent:cajun_brian}
        \item[] \longtrans{Brian (Cajun): Me, I say a Cajun, that's people who came from Canada.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Creoles, likewise, could point to this story when defining Cajuns.
      However, it may have only become so pervasive more recently.
      As explained by Bruce, born in \Sexpr{participants[participants$Name == "Bruce Wiltz", "Birth Year"]}, this was not a story that he heard in his youth.

      Most often, participants determined Acadian ancestry through family names, as in Sentence \ref{sent:cajun_jack} where Jack uses an alter's family name to conclude that he is Cajun.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Jack (Cajun): Wilton, il est cadien.
              Il est Chenevert.
              Chenevert, c'est un mot très très cadien. (54:17)\label{sent:cajun_jack}
        \item[] \longtrans{Jack (Cajun): Wilton, he's Cajun.
              He's a Chenevert.
              Chenevert, that's a very very Cajun word.}
      \end{enumerate}
      This case is particularly notable because Chenevert was the family name of two participants in this study: Randal, a White Cajun, and Rachel.
      Despite how strongly linked Jack considered Chenevert to be to Cajun ancestry, Rachel did not identify as Cajun but rather as a Black Creole.
      
      This incongruency did not come up during this exchange with Jack, but it did often come up when Cajuns were assigning ethnicities to their alters, as in Sentence \ref{sent:cajun_lillian1} where Lillian's thought process around her husband's ethnicity is spoken out loud.      
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Lillian (Cajun): Il est allemand, mais il est blanc, mais il parle cadien.
              Il dit, je crois lui il dit il est cadien, mais il connaît son ancêtres sont allemands. (39:13)\label{sent:cajun_lillian1}
        \item[] \longtrans{Lillian (Cajun): He's German, but he's White, but he speaks Cajun.
              He says, I believe that he says that he's Cajun, but he knows that his ancestors are German.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Lillian acknowledged that her husbands ancestors were not Acadian but rather German.
      However, German being a European and prototypically White category helped her arrive at the conclusion that he should be considered Cajun.
      This was in fact one of only two times when a Cajun participant explicitly connected Cajun identity to Whiteness rather than signaling Whiteness through European categories such as German, Spanish, or French.
      Even Acadian itself is ultimately a way to connect back to European categories as they were initially colonists from France.

      Lillian's language ideology comes through with also describing her husband as speaking \lexi{cadien} \gloss{Cajun} rather than using a term that does not directly connect it to a local ethnicity.
      Presumably, as both she and her husband grew up in Vermilion Parish southwest of Lafayette where Louisiana Creole has not been formally attested, he would also use the same Cajun-marked subject pronouns that Lillian used exclusively.
      Her statement thus reproduced the idea that this is a Cajun way of speaking.

      Creoles were not silent when defining Cajuns, but they had much less to say than Cajuns did, a curtness that did not apply to Cajuns when defining Creoles.
      Some Creoles had Whiteness more at the forefront of their thoughts when defining Cajuns, as seen in Gene and James' statements in Sentences \ref{sent:cajun_gene} and \ref{sent:cajun_james}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Gene (Creole): Un Cadien?
              That's a White man. (43:56)\label{sent:cajun_gene}
        \item[] \longtrans{Gene (Creole): A Cajun?
              That's a White man.}
        \item James (Creole): Les Cadiens, okay, c'est les Blancs.
              Ils ont d'héritage de Nova Scotia. (41:27)\label{sent:cajun_james}
        \item[] \longtrans{James (Creole): Cajuns okay, those are Whites.
              They have a heritage from Nova Scotia.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Gene was generally quite blunt in discussing race, and in this case, he may have switched to English to be even more plain and emphatic about his understanding of Cajuns.
      James, on the other hand, was mild-mannered and had a sort of scholarly side to him, having been a priest and a teacher, so while he immediately highlighted the connection between Cajuns and Whiteness, he also repeated the Cajun origin story, implicitly tying Acadians themselves to Whiteness in the process.

      While this forthright construction of Cajuns as White was not common, especially in the discourse of Cajuns, the same cannot be said of the construction of Creoles as Black.
      For Cajun participants, even in cases where they were focusing on historical constructions of Creoles, they invariably landed on more explicit connections of Blackness, as in Sentence \ref{sent:creole_lucille}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Lucille (Cajun): La Ville Platte, la paroisse Évangéline, a été vraiment fondée par beaucoup de soldats de Napoléon, ... donc c'est vraiment, c'est créole dans le sens de créole, pas dans le sens de couleur de la peau.
              C'était, you know, les enfants des colons. (39:10)\label{sent:creole_lucille}
        \item[] \longtrans{Lucille (Cajun): Ville Platte, Evangeline Parish, was really founded by a lot of Napoleon's soldiers, ... so it's really, it's Creole in the sense of Creole, not in the sense of skin color.
        That was, you know, the children of colonists.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Lucille contrasted her understanding of Creoles as descending from French colonists with that which she assumed would be my understanding of Creoles as Black, which speaks to the pervasiveness of the association of Creoles with Blackness given that I had not expressed this view during the interview.

      Cajuns indeed appeared to not want to always go directly to Blackness when defining Creoles.
      Prefacing what were ultimately fairly direct connections that they made between Creoles and Blackness with historical trends in the definition may have been done to soften statements that could be seen as inflammatory, a tactic that was expanded further in Sentence \ref{sent:creole_randal1}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Randal (Cajun): Alentour ici, je connais pas si t'as perçu ça ou tu connais ça, asteur ça a venu, it evolved into un Creole c'est quelqu'un noir. (58:46)\label{sent:creole_randal1}
        \item[] \longtrans{Randal (Cajun): Around here, I don't know if you noticed this or you know this, now that became, it evolved into a Creole is someone Black.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Randal not only hinted at the fact that the definition he was about to give was not always how Creoles were defined, but he also code-switched to English in that moment.
      It may have been that Randal simply did not have an F\&C word for \lexi{evolved}, but his fluency was general excellent.
      He used F\&C frequently and even internationally through a number of connections he had with language activists in Canada.
      If he did have a way of expressing the idea of evolving in F\&C, then, the other explanation for this switch is that it may have been another way to soften the statement, to create distance with it relative to the conversation that was taking place in F\&C.
              
      Not all explicit associations that Cajuns made between Creoles and Blackness were softened, however, as can be seen in Sentence \ref{sent:creole_dennis}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Dennis (Cajun): Well, on usait pas ce mot \lexi{créole} dans ce temps-là.
              Non, c'était des uhm, on juste disait du monde noir ou des nègres\footnote{
                This term and its translation will be censored due to their status as slurs.
              }, mais le propre nom, c'était des Créoles noirs. (38:06)\label{sent:creole_dennis}
        \item[] \longtrans{Dennis (Cajun): Well, we didn't use this word \lexi{Creole} at that time.
              No, that was uhm, we would just say Black people or ******, but the proper name, that's Black Creoles.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Dennis was asked to provide his definition of Creoles, to which he immediately explained that the prevelance of this term in modern discourse was a recent phenomenon.
      Indeed, Judy, another Cajun participant, also expressed that she did not begin hearing the term \lexi{Creole} until relatively recently.
      The terms that Dennis cited as being used in his younger days for Creoles were all squarely centered on Blackness, including \lexi{n****}, a slur which will be discussed in more detail farther down.
      Even the ``proper name'' for Creoles that Dennis provided, \lexi{Créoles noirs} \gloss{Black Creoles}, heavily foregrounded Blackness.
      This sort of explicitness was not uncommon when Cajuns discussed Creoles.

      Creoles themselves, on the other hand, spoke more in terms of mixing when defining Creoles.
      This can be seen in Sentence \ref{sent:creole_floyd} where Floyd explains his understanding of how that mixing originally came about.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Floyd (Creole): Dans les temps, les vieux, c'était quand les Blancs et puis la Couleur taient vini ensemble et fait un petit, okay?
              C'était pas un bonne affaire pour les Couleurs.
              Dans ces temps-ça-là, yé taient travailler dans le clos, et puis yé taient prendre les femmes, les Couleurs, et puis, to connais, fait yé't'oler fait avec yé. (45:55)\label{sent:creole_floyd}
        \item[] \longtrans{Floyd (Creole): In the past, the old people, that was when Whites and the Colored were coming together and making a little one, okay?
              That wasn't a good thing for the Coloreds.
              At that time, they would work in the field, and then they would take the women, the Coloreds, and then, you know, do what they wanted to do with them.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Two parts of this explanation are notable.
      The first is that Floyd decided to highlight the suffering and hardships that Creoles have faced.
      For him, understanding Creoles was not as simple as understanding a definition but required an understanding of the social context in which they have existed.
      The second is that the sort of mixing that Floyd discussing involved Blackness.
      In focusing on mixing, Creoles were perhaps aiming to mitigate the stigma that society has attached to Blackness, but in always implicating Black people in that mixing, as all the Creole participants did, they were perhaps also aiming to maintain solidarity with racialized Black people of the broader US.
      This nuancing is something of which Cajuns had no need when they discussed Creoles, hence, even when Cajuns softened their definitions of Creoles by acknowledging historical definitions, those historical definitions were not part of their modern definitions.
      If Creoles of the past were locals or the offspring of European colonists, that had no bearing on their understanding of Creoles as simply Black today.
      For Creoles themselves, though, Blackness is associated with the category today because it was associated with the category in the past.
      They are the descendants of these past Creoles rather than a completely modern construction.
      % A slip that nevertheless aligns Louisiana Creole with Creoles
        % Brian (Cajun): Dans la guerre civile, avant la guerre civile, ça voulait pas, les Blancs voulaient pas être mêlés avec les Noirs, ça fait ils ont commencé dire si tu parles créole, ou bien si tu es créole, tu es noir, et puis si tu es blanc, tu es cadien ou bien américain. (57:51)  

      Creoles often highlighted culture, as well, in defining themselves, although there was some disagreement on this point.
      Sentences \ref{sent:creole_terry} and \ref{sent:creole_owen} exemplify the contrast in views.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Terry (Creole): C'est une race de monde mêlé.
              Inaura plus qu'une qualité de Créole, parce que, tu vois, le monde qui parle créole, ils ont du monde qui deviennent les West Indies, Haiti. (41:49)\label{sent:creole_terry}
        \item[] \longtrans{Terry (Creole): It's a race of mixed people.
              There will be more than one type of Creole, because, you see, the people who speak Creole, they have people who come from the West Indies, Haiti.}
        \item Owen (Creole): Mo crois, je crois quand même, les gens de Natchitoches, on a parlé avec un groupe des gens qui dit qu'ils sont les descendants des Français, qui disent qu'ils sont créoles, et on a demandé, ``Mais, qu'est-ce que ça veut dire, ça?''
              Parce que c'est vraiment, ce n'est pas une race.
              C'est plutôt une culture. (51:42)\label{sent:creole_owen}
        \item[] \longtrans{Owen (Creole): I believe, I believe even, the people of Natchitoches, we spoke with a group of people who say that they're descendants of the French, who say that they're Creole, and we asked, ``Well, what does that mean?''
              Because it's really, it's not a race.
              Rather, it's a culture.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Terry plainly viewed Creoles as mixed, but the result of that mixture for him was a distinct racialized group.
      Indeed, Terry was one of only two Creoles who gave Creole\footnote{
        One other Creole, James, did include Creole in his racialized identity, but it was one of two parts, the other being African American.
      } as their racialized identity, akin to a border identity \parencite{holliday_intonational_2016, rockquemore_race_1999, susberry_racial_2004}.
      Owen, on the other hand, was just as plain in his assertion that Creole is a culture.

      Two aspects of Owen's statements are worth exploring further.
      One is the role of place.
      Owen has spent most of his life in Breaux Bridge, just east of Lafayette.
      The town he discussed, Natchitoches, is roughly 150 miles northwest of Breaux Bridge, beyond the official Acadiana parishes even.
      That he cited this group not only puts physical distance between his view and theirs, but it also provides context relevant to Terry's physical position.
      Terry has spent his life in the Swords/Mallet area, roughly 35 miles northwest of Breaux Bridge.
      While not nearly as distant, Terry's view appears more in line with those of the people of Natchitoches.
      It is not out of the question that the life experiences of Creoles from different parts of Louisiana may lead them to construct their own identities differently.
      This was in fact exactly the sort of phenomenon that \textcite{wendte_tale_2020} explored for Creoles in Texas and Louisiana.

      The other notable aspect of Owen's statements is that he switched his subject pronoun system to discuss the matter from his more frequent use of Creole-marked pronouns to Cajun-marked pronouns, the latter being the subject pronouns that Terry used exclusively.
      Owen began with \sent{mo crois} \gloss{I believe}, but then corrected himself with \sent{je crois} \gloss{I believe}, spending the rest of the exchange in this latter Cajun-marked mode.
      The effect emphasizes the distance between himself and those Creoles of Natchitoches, perhaps for the sake of emphasizing to what degree he disagrees with them.
      Incidentally, this also creates distance between Owen and those who speak with Cajun-marked pronouns by juxtaposing a view that he opposes with this way of speaking.
      Those who use Cajun-marked pronouns would of course mostly be Cajuns, but in this case, that group also include Terry.
      
      To the extent that Creoles do define themselves culturally, their role in local culture products and practices has often been diminished or even erased.
      Much of this situation has already been shown in the literature \parencite{giancarlo_dont_2019}, but it was reproduced in the current data, as well, as in Sentence \ref{sent:cajun_lucille}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Lucille (Cajun): Y avait pas de français cadien ou créole.
              Y avait le français.
              C'était la musique en français.
              C'était pas la musique cadienne, you know? (40:16)\label{sent:cajun_lucille}
        \item[] \longtrans{Lucille (Cajun): There was no Cajun or Creole French.
              There was French.
              It was music in French.
              It wasn't Cajun music, you know?}
      \end{enumerate}
      What Lucille was pointing out in these statements is that not only were the terms \lexi{Cajun} and \lexi{Creole} not used in branding cultural products and practices in her youth, she was also implicitly acknowledging that \lexi{Cajun} has become the preferred branding, so much so that Deborah identified as a White Creole in part as a rejection of how pervasive Cajun branding is.
      Creoles have, however, been directly involved in the development of the local culture.
      One of the earliest stars and most influential musicians in the genre that Lucille discussed above was Amédé Ardoin, typically described as a Black Creole \parencite[p.~73]{ancelet_cajun_1999}.
      The Creole category being constructed as lesser than Cajun has made it a less attractive choice for broad marketing, though.
      Just as White hegemony is constructed in the broader US, there are signs of a constructed Cajun hegemony in Louisiana.

      Florence provided statements that neatly summed up the social positions of Creoles and Cajuns in Louisiana in Sentence \ref{sent:life_florence2} where she began by discussing the position of Cajuns.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Florence (Creole): Yé la vie c'était pas comme no-tiens.
              Yé taient gain un petit brin plus que nous-autres des fois, parce que yé taient aider un et l'autre un tas, et là navait les Blancs.
              C'est pas les mêmes que les Cadiens, okay?
              Un Blanc, yé taient gain un tas.
              Un Cadien tait pas gain un tas comme un Blanc, so les Cadiens taient travailler un tas pour des Blancs, parce que yé taient vini de Canada.
              Yé taient pas vini en haut dans ça.
              Yé yé taient vini l'autre bord, so quand yé vini, c'est yé taient pauvres.
              Yé taient proche aussi pauvres qu'un Créole.
              Proche.
              Mais, yé taient gain un petit brin plus, parce que yé saient aider les Blancs ...
              Mais, un Cadien, ... yé saient pas aider un Créole much, pas un tas, mais un Blanc sait aider un Créole plus. (82:44)\label{sent:life_florence2}
        \item[] \longtrans{Florence (Creole): Their life wasn't like ours.
              They had a little bit more than us sometimes, because they helped one another a lot, and then there were the White people.
              That's not the same as the Cajuns, okay?
              A White person, they had a lot.
              A Cajun didn't have a lot like a White person, so Cajuns worked a lot for White people, because they came from Canada.
              They didn't come from up high in that.
              They came from the other side, so when they came, they were poor.
              They were almost as poor as a Creole.
              Almost.
              But, they had a little bit more, because they would help the White people ...
              But, a Cajun, ... they wouldn't help a Creole much, not a lot, but a White person would help a Creole more.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Florence's description is reminiscent of that which was discussed in Section \ref{sec:united_states} where a group achieves an insecure middle status in a constructed social hierarchy.
      Here, Cajuns are not exactly White, but they were more amenable to those who were White but not Cajun.
      They could in effect work with this latter group to improve their lot, but that work meant marginalizing Creoles in order for Cajuns to keep their status.
      White people, whose status was not insecure, did not need to directly marginalize Creoles as long as Cajuns did that for them.
      This understanding of the constructed social order in Louisiana is not necessarily shared by all Louisianians, of course, but it is generally in accord with what is suggested by the discourse of the current study's participants.

        % Speaking French or even showing an appreciation for French was important for Cajun identity
          % For Cajuns, doesn't matter what the French sounds like either
            % Elizabeth: Geraldine, pas cadienne. Marks. Li était élevée à Crowley. Ses parents c'était pas, c'était Italian. Okay. Marks is her maiden name. Mo crois pas li parle français. (50:14) [not just an Italian family name but doesn't speak French]
            % \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
            %   \item Rose (Cajun): Out of the three, she, yeah, she's my strongest one, yeah because she sends her kids to French immersion.
            %         That says it all. (100:54)\label{sent:cajun_rose}
            % \end{enumerate}

    \subsection{One-directional imitation}
      Owen's statements in Sentence \ref{sent:creole_owen} above already suggested a level of awareness that there are different ways of speaking F\&C, but meta-linguistic commentary by the participants addressed this more directly.
      While there was generally widespread agreement among participants that Creoles and Cajuns speak differently, for Cajuns, this was a very clear distinction that is as tied up with Blackness as the Creole category is for them, whereas Creoles were in some ways more ambivalent about the distinction.
      This difference in how each group approached the subject is again indicative of a constructed social hierarchy where emphasing differences between the groups is rather inconsequential for Cajuns with regard to their life outcomes, whereas Creoles can less afford to be singled out as different despite their general pride in their identities.

      Floyd presented an example of downplaying the difference in the ways each group speaks in Sentence \ref{sent:floyd_casse}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Floyd (Creole): Le français et le créole, c'est la même affaire.
              C'est juste un petit peu cassé. (69:14)\label{sent:floyd_casse}
        \item[] \longtrans{Floyd (Creole): French and Creole, it's the same thing.
              It's just a bit broken.}
      \end{enumerate}
      When Floyd stated in that ``it's'' broken, he was referring to both the way Cajuns and the way Creoles speak in Louisiana, comparing them to how French is spoken outside of Louisiana.
      For him, a Creole who uses Creole-marked pronouns, minimizing the distance between Cajuns and Creoles is perhaps a way to prevent the marginalization of his speech and identity.
        
      When Creoles acknowledged differences in how Cajuns speak from themselves, this was generally contextualized by framing it as a need to understand Cajuns.
      Tracy was particularly clear about this need in Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_tracy}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Tracy (Creole): To peux trapper la manière yé parlent, comme la manière yé parlent, ça yé a dit, to vois?
              Un Cadien, to gain pour apprendre comment li parle, then si to peux trapper ça, then to connais où to restes. (89:09)\label{sent:different_speech_tracy}
        \item[] \longtrans{Tracy (Creole): You can pick up the way they speak, like the way they speak, what they will say, you see?
              A Cajun, you have to learn how he speaks, then if you can pick that up, then you know where you stand.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Tracy explained that understanding what Cajuns are saying helps one know how one is viewed by Cajuns, something that would be of great importance for those who may be marginalized by Cajuns.
      But this concern was not entirely shared by other Creoles, a Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_rachel} shows.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Rachel (Creole): Les mondes, yé vient.
              Y'apè causer. ...
              It's a lot of White people come and say, you know, they speaking Cajun a lot of time, but some I don't really understand too good, Cajun sometime, but I let them know.
              I said, ``I don't speak Cajun. I'm a Creole.'' ...
              But sometime, ... na des fois n'a causer jusqu'à n'a comprendre un à l'autre, you know? (22:16)\label{sent:different_speech_rachel}
        \item[] \longtrans{Rachel (Creole): People, they come.
              They're talking. ...
              It's a lot of White people come and say, you know, they speak Cajun a lot of time, but some I don't really understand too good, Cajun sometime, but I let them know.
              I said, ``I don't speak Cajun. I'm Creole.'' ...
              But sometime, ... there are times we will talk until we will understand one another, you know?}
      \end{enumerate}
      Rachel did not express animosity over these situations, but she seemed to accept that, if she does not understand a Cajun, it does not necessarily create any risks for her.
      What she also did in these statements was connect ways of speaking F\&C to the categories of Cajun and Creole.
      Explaining that she does not speak ``Cajun'' because she is Creole is something of a naturalizing statement that makes Cajun-marked speech inherently part of Cajun people and likewise for Creole-marked speech and Creole people.
      % Samantha seemed to be coming from another world completely in terms of how she addressed mutual comprehension with Cajuns (and I think her Creole family, too?)
        % (51:55) [seeing herself as someone who speaks more like Europeans, gives an example of the difference]
        % Samantha: Par exemple, je sais le mot pour bouger en cajun est grouille-toi ou grouille, et il y a un mot que les personnes en français et les personne qui parlent le cajun savent. C'est raton laveur. Ouaouaron, ils dit en cajun, et je ne sais pas si c'est la même chose en le français européen.    
          
      Not present in the above Creoles' acknowledgements that Cajuns may speak differently from them are imitations of Cajun speech.
      In fact, Gene was more forthcoming with imitations of how Creoles from places other than his region speak, as seen in Sentence \ref{sent:imitation_gene}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Gene (Creole): Yé parlent différent.
              Là, comme à Lafayette, Carencro, Scott, ça't'apè fait tous les mêmes affaires, mais tu gone couru par d'autres, c'est quelque chose d'autre. ...
              Ouais là, toi tu vas dis `let's go' uh, ``Mais je vais à la grocerie.'' ... ``Mo couru la grocerie.'' (42:15)\label{sent:imitation_gene}
        \item[] \longtrans{Gene (Creole): They speak differently.
              There, like in Lafayette, Caraencro, Scott, they're doing all the same things, but you leave to go other places, that's something else. ...
              Yeah there, you're going to say `let's go' uh, ``Well I'm going to the grocery store.'' ... ``I went to the grocery store.''}
      \end{enumerate}
      Gene himself was the only speaker who used a mix of Cajun-marked and Creole-marked subject pronouns consistently, preferring Cajun-marked pronouns for all pronoun types other than 3pl.
      He not only demonstrates in this passage that he can use different 1sg subject pronouns in his imitations, \lexi{je} and \lexi{mo}, but one of only \convertSmallNumber{\Sexpr{tablesParticipant$thirdPl$`Gene Delcambre`["ça"] + tablesParticipant$thirdPl$`Gene Delcambre`["ils"]}} tokens of 3pl that he produced that were not \lexi{yé} is juxtaposed with a token of \lexi{yé}.
      It is not entirely clear why he might have produced both \lexi{yé} and \lexi{ça} for 3pl here, but he was perhaps thinking ahead to the idea of giving examples of different ways of speaking and so produced different subject pronouns in his non-imitation speech.
      Regardless, Gene did not frame his utterances as imitations of Cajuns but rather of Creoles while also highlighting the importance of place.

      A Creole with a very different perspective on this topic was Deborah.
      Much like Floyd, instead of differences between Cajuns and Creoles, she focused on comparing F\&C in Louisiana to French outside of Louisiana in Sentence \ref{sent:roi_pan}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Deborah (White Creole): Tu vois la différence ici? International French et Creole French. ... [reads Creole-marked language] ... Et couru-vini, c'est pire que ça. (50:45)
      \end{enumerate}
      Deborah was in fact relying on the authority of a book of stories from Creole speakers that were collected at the end of the 19th century.
      She characterized this as ``Creole French'' and misunderstood much of what was written, which is reproduced here as-is in Sentence \ref{sent:roi_pan}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item In fois yavait in madame qui té si joli, si joli, qué li té jamin oulé marié. \parencite[p.~56]{fortier_louisiana_2011}\label{sent:roi_pan}
        \item[] \longtrans{Once there was a woman who was so pretty, so pretty, that she never wanted to marry.}
      \end{enumerate}
      The line that she read indeed included the Creole-marked subject pronoun \lexi{li}.
      Similar to Floyd, she saw this as inferior to ``International French'', and she used a scholarly work to speak for her in that evaluation.
      It is doubtful that she would have evaluated Floyd's speech positively, however, given his similar use of Creole-marked pronouns.
      Deborah did not actually give an evaluation of Cajun-marked speech like her own, and she may not have even seen her own speech as Cajun-like given her distaste for the term \lexi{Cajun}.
      She appeared to construct her own identity as a White Creole in relation to the Creole aristocracy of the Creole myth (see Section \ref{sec:creole_history}), distinct from both Cajuns and other modern-day Creoles.

      Cajuns voluntarily gave far more examples of what they perceived to be the speech patterns of Creoles.
      Cynthia's example from Sentence \ref{sent:creole_meta} is reproduced here in Sentence \ref{sent:imitation_cynthia}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Cynthia (Cajun): I went to the store.
              ``Mo tais couru au store.'' (45:03)\label{sent:imitation_cynthia}
        \item[] \longtrans{Cynthia (Cajun): I went to the store.
              ``I would go to the store.''}
      \end{enumerate}
      Cynthia not only employed a Creole-marked subject pronoun for 1sg in this phrase, unlike her own preference for Cajun-marked \lexi{je}, but she also chose to use an English-origin lexical item, perhaps to signal an ideology that says that this way of speaking is not quite French.
      It is also notable that she included \lexi{tais}, which in this case would more likely express imperfective past rather than the perfective past that she gave in her translation of the phrase.
      It is not clear whether she was simply unfamiliar with exactly how such ``markers'' are used or went too far in trying to emphasize what she saw as unique features of Creoles' speech.

      Austin also produced an interesting imitation of Creole-marked speech in Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_austin}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Austin (Cajun): Elle vient de Henderson, et puis to ca' dis li parle un petit brin créole là. (81:02)\label{sent:different_speech_austin}
        \item[] \longtrans{Austin (Cajun): She's from Henderson, and so you can say that she speaks a bit of Creole there.}
      \end{enumerate}
      The first clause uses the Cajun-marked 3sg.F pronoun \lexi{elle} before the rest of the utterance switches to the Creole-marked pronouns \lexi{to} (2sg.T) and \lexi{li} (3sg.F).
      In this case, Austin was not necessarily producing this imitation to show how Creole people speak but rather to show how people from Henderson speak as he did not identify this woman as Creole or Cajun or anything else.

      Place repeatedly came up during conversations when Cajuns addressed the topic of the speech of Creoles.
      Brian proceeded to speak about different towns in Sentence \ref{sent:imitation_brian} when asked if people in the community view Creoles as Black and Cajuns as White.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Brian (Cajun): Ina un tas du monde qui dit ça, parce que, tu vois, par exemple en Cecilia, la langue là-bas c'est plus créole, ``mo gain, to gain,'' les mots comme   ça, ``couru, mo couru, to couru,'' et puis Arnaudville, c'est cadien ou bien français. ...
              Ça fait, le français à Arnaudville, ina un tas du monde va dire c'est un, j'aime pas user le mot, mais c'est un meilleur français. (59:34)\label{sent:imitation_brian}
        \item[] \longtrans{??}
      \end{enumerate}
      Brian was not asked in this instance about the language of Creoles and Cajuns but rather if those categories are considered to be associated with racialized categories from the broader US, but this question seemed to have triggered him to think about Creole-marked speech anyway.
      His examples of such speech all consisted of a Creole-marked subject pronoun and a verb that commonly appears in descriptive linguistic work on Louisiana Creole, neither of which were present in Brian's own F\&C.
      Despite the fact that he explicitly made this about place by associating such speech with the town of Cecilia, the way Blackness and Whiteness triggered his statements suggests that they are also associated with ways of speaking in his mind.

      Bruce used similar linguistic features in imitating the way Louisiana Creole in Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_bruce2}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Bruce (Cajun): Et eux, ils ont venu de autour de Catahoula, et ça parlait un peu de le créole aussi, eh?
            ``Mo gain li, to gain li,'' et c'est la seule fois moi j'avais entendu ça. (46:21)\label{sent:different_speech_bruce2}
        \item[] \longtrans{Bruce (Cajun): And them, they came from around Catahoula, and they spoke a bit of Creole also, eh?
              ``I have it, you have it,'' and that's the only time that I had heard that.}
      \end{enumerate}
      This was, however, an explanation of how some Cajuns from Catahoula speak rather than how Creoles speak, but as with Brian, there was a clear association with Blackness in this way of speaking regardless.
      Moments later, he followed this imitation up with Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_bruce1}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Bruce (Cajun): J'ai entendu dire c'était le français n****. (68:09)\label{sent:different_speech_bruce1}
        \item[] \longtrans{Bruce (Cajun): I've heard it said that it was ***** French.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Presenting this as something that others have said allows Bruce to distance himself from such a pejorative and racializing statement, but if his personal view on the validity of that description cannot be ascertained, one can at least see that the association is present in his mind.

      As can be seen, Cajuns more readily acknowledged different ways of speaking F\&C than Creoles and either explicitly or implicitly associated Creole-marked speech with Creoles and/or Blackness.
      Cajuns offered ample imitations of Creole-marked speech that always included the subject pronouns that were found to be associated with Creoles in Chapter \ref{chap:pronoun_variation}.
      This shows an awareness of the differences in the general tendencies for Creoles' and Cajuns' use of F\&C, but evaluative statements and Creoles' reticence to discuss Cajuns' speech in the same ways is suggestive of a lower position for Creoles in a constructed social hierarchy.
      The discussions of place also indicate that this participants are aware that Creoles do not always use Creole-marked pronouns and Cajuns do not always use Cajun-marked pronouns.
      This potential for place to be an important predictor of F\&C subject pronouns is congruent for what was seen in Chapter \ref{chap:networks} for those participants who did not follow the general tendencies for subject pronouns relative to ethnicity.
    
    \subsection{Distance and closeness}
      Imitations did not account for all instances of participants using subject pronouns that were not part of their most consistently used system.
      Other cases more likely represented speakers attempting to express various social meanings through the indexical values of different pronouns.
      Cajun-marked pronouns sometimes signaled formality, learnedness, historical distance, or social distance from Creoles.
      Contrary to their regular use in imitations, Creole-marked pronouns were rarely exploited as part of intra-speaker variation.
      Some of the instances where this did occur, though, may have signaled a distinct sort of localness that Cajun-marked pronouns could not quite achieve.
      
      The potential for indexing a sort of formality with Cajun-marked subject pronouns may already be evident from the effect that exposure to institutional French was found to have on subject pronoun realizations in Chapter \ref{chap:pronoun_variation}.
      In short, Cajun-marked pronouns more closely resemble what is learned in a French-language classroom.
      Despite efforts to make sociolinguistic interviews feel casual and comfortable, they will perhaps always have some level of formality to them for study participants.
      This is a possible explanation for Elizabeth's speech patterns at the beginning of her interview, as seen in Sentence \ref{sent:switch_elizabeth}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Elizabeth (Cajun): Non, j'ai juste été née là.
              Mo n- toujours restais, habitais, à Henderson.
              C'est là mo tais élevée. (2:14)\label{sent:switch_elizabeth}
        \item[] \longtrans{Elizabeth (Cajun): No, I was just born there.
              I n- lived, lived, in Henderson.
              That's where I was raised.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Elizabeth was clarifying her response to a question about where she was born, that being Arnaudville.
      Her token here of \lexi{je} for 1sg was one of only three Cajun-marked pronouns she used for any pronoun type during the interview, one of the other two occurring immediately before these utterances.
      She quickly switched to the Creole-marked \lexi{mo} at this very early point and never reverted back.
      Given my presence as an academic with a voice recorder, she may have momentarily felt that this was almost an evaluation of her F\&C and decided that Cajun-marked pronouns would be more appropriate.
      Indeed, later in the interview, she was asked if the excursion tours she led as a profession were ever conducted in F\&C, to which she replied that she did not feel confident enough in the language to conduct tours in F\&C.
      It is doubtful that her fluency was truly an issue, however, as she was very capable of expressing herself on a number of topics, but rather she may have felt some level of insecurity about the form of her speech.
      Such an insecurity would also play into beginning her interview with subject pronouns of which participants often showed higher evaluations than her own.

      A participant with a similar linguistic background who, in contrast, did not express the faintest hint of linguistic insecurity was Errol.
      Like Elizabeth, he was another Cajun who used Creole-marked subject pronouns almost exclusively.
      Also like Elizabeth, he began the interview with two tokens of \lexi{je} rather than his typical \lexi{mo} for 1sg.
      A formality effect may have been at play in his case, as well, but he was also more willing to employ Cajun-marked pronouns for discursive work, as in Sentence \ref{sent:creole_errol2}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Errol (Cajun): Mo mo l'aime le manger créole. ...\label{sent:creole_errol2}
        \item[] Joshua (Interviewer): Vous avez un plat préféré qui est créole?
        \item[] Errol: To peux faire gombo.
              To peux fais étouffé, mais uh
        \item[] Joshua: Ça c'est créole?
        \item[] Errol: Un gombo, ouais.
              Yé cuit ça différent, tu connais?
              Cadiens fait gombo itou, mais les Créoles fait un différent gombo.
        \item[] Joshua: Quoi c'est la différence?
        \item[] Errol: Well, ça yé met dans le gombo.
              Mais, peut-être un Cadien va cuit un gombo avec du roux puis la viande.
              Un Créole va peut-être fait un gombo avec des tomates dans le roux. ...
        \item[] Joshua: C'est bon comme ça?
        \item[] Errol: Mo pas connais.
              Mo mo je l'aime pas ça.
        \item[] \longtrans{Errol (Cajun): I like Creole food. ...}
        \item[] \longtrans{Joshua (Interviewer): Do you have a favorite dish that's Creole?}
        \item[] \longtrans{Errol: You can make gumbo.
              You can make étouffé, but uh}
        \item[] \longtrans{Joshua: That's Creole?}
        \item[] \longtrans{Errol: A gumbo, yeah.
              They cook that differently, you know?
              Cajuns make gumbo, too, but Creoles make a different gumbo.}
        \item[] \longtrans{Joshua: What's the difference?}
        \item[] \longtrans{Errol: Well, what they put in the gumbo.
              Well, maybe a Cajun will cook a gumbo with some roux and meat.
              A Creole will maybe make a gumbo with tomatoes in the roux. ...}
        \item[] \longtrans{Joshua: Is it good like that?}
        \item[] \longtrans{Errol: I don't know.
              I don't like that.}
      \end{enumerate}
      This exchange is printed here at greater length because it shows a rather rapid evolution of Errol's stance towards Creole cuisine.
      He initially expresses an appreciation for Creole dishes, but as the conversation became more detailed, it became clear that there was not a strong distinction between what he considered to be Creole dishes and what he considered to be Cajun dishes.
      Primarily, one ingredient, tomatoes, made all the difference.
      Finally, when asking if gumbo made by Creoles with tomatoes tasted good, Errol suddenly shifted his initial appreciative stance to claiming ignorance and dislike of such dishes.
      In this shift, his consistent use of Creole-marked \lexi{mo} also shifted to Cajun-marked \lexi{je}, perhaps in an effort to distance himself even farther from Creoles in his final stance shift.
      Errol's ability to use \lexi{je}, the apparent indexicality it has for him, and his apparent apprehension about being too closely associated with Creoles make it initially difficult to see why he preferred Creole-marked pronouns.
      This will be addressed again farther down.

      Creoles who typically used Creole-marked speech -- but not those who typically used Cajun-marked speech -- also exploited subject pronouns for their indexicality or for discursive effects.
      Floyd perhaps produced an example of the former in Sentence \ref{sent:ils_floyd}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Floyd (Creole): Les vieux mondes, yé proche tous, ils sont partis, to connais? (13:10)\label{sent:ils_floyd}
        \item[] \longtrans{Floyd (Creole): The old folks, they're almost all, they're gone, you know?}
      \end{enumerate}
      This utterance contains one of Floyd's three tokens of \lexi{ils} for 3pl during his interview.
      Notably, it came immediately after the Creole-marked \lexi{yé}, possibly as a correction, although that is not terribly clear.
      What is clear is that this token of \lexi{ils} was situated in the midst of many tokens of Creole-marked pronouns, \lexi{to} for 2sg.T in the same utterance being another.
      In this statement as well as one of the others where he used \lexi{ils}, he is referring to older family members who have passed away, leaving him with no one with whom he can speak F\&C.
      In contrast, shortly afterward when speaking about younger people, he produced \sent{yé parlent pas} \gloss{they do not speak}.
      These juxtapositions of \lexi{ils} with older people and \lexi{yé} with younger people are perhaps suggest that Cajun-marked pronouns index distance in time for Floyd.
      This can only be a tentative explanation, however, as much of the conversation involved discussing the past using \lexi{yé}, but this explanation is in line with Errol's creation of social distance with Cajun-marked pronouns, and other Creoles likewise seemed to possibly be indexing types of distance with Cajun-marked pronouns.

      One of those other Creoles was Owen.
      As was already seen in Sentence \ref{sent:creole_owen}, Owen was quite comfortable alternating his subject pronouns even for extended periods of time.
      Although he showed a preference for Creole-marked pronouns, his interview contained considerable usage of Cajun-marked pronouns, as well.
      An example of a length section containing Cajun-marked pronouns can be seen in Sentence \ref{sent:life_owen}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Owen (Creole): J'ai été sélectionné pour un poste à [a high school] comme assistant principal, ça fait j'ai pris ça, mais j'ai commencé là, mais j'ai pas resté là trop longtemps.
              Non, on a décidé que je dois travailler à [a different high school].
              Je crois que c'était à cause de ma race.
              À ce temps-là, il faut avoir quand même un Noir. (22:53)\label{sent:life_owen}
        \item[] \longtrans{Owen (Creole): I was selected for a post at [a high school] as assistant principal, so I took that, but I started there, but I didn't stay there too long.
              No, someone decided that I have to work at [a different high school].
              I believe that that was because of my race.
              At that time, it was necessary to have at least one Black person.}
      \end{enumerate}
      In the previous example from Owen, he seemed to use Cajun-marked pronouns to signal distance from the Creoles in Natchitoches, who were in fact physically distant from him as well, or perhaps to slightly mock their idea that Creole is a race.
      In this current example, the topic is a sensitive one that revolves around an educational setting where a more formal language is expected, but the people involved are also those from whom Owen feels socially distant.
      Both are plausible explanations for why he chose to switch to Cajun-marked pronouns here, and many of the other instances where he did so were similar.
      They by and large included formal settings, distant people, and ideas that were in opposition to his own.
      
      One of the subject pronouns produced by Owen above was \lexi{on} for impersonal, which was in fact rare for both Creole and Cajun participants, occurring only \Sexpr{nrow(tokensAll[tokensAll$ProType == "imp" & tokensAll$ProUnder == "on",])} times in the entire corpus.
      The rarity of this use of \lexi{on} may give it more of a foreign, or distant, indexical value than a Cajun one.
      This is likewise a plausible interpretation for Dennis's pronoun choice in Sentence \ref{sent:nous_dennis} when he voices the original Acadian immigrants to Louisiana.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Dennis (Cajun): Et quand ils sont arrivés, ils ont présenté comme, ``Nous sommes des Acadiens,'' et les Espagnols et le monde à Nouvelle-Orléans avaient du tracas à dire Acadien, ça fait ça disait Cadien. (24:30)\label{sent:nous_dennis}
        \item[] \longtrans{And when the arrived, they presented themselves like, ``We're Acadians,'' and the Spaniards and the people in New Orleans had trouble saying Acadian, so they would say Cajun.}
      \end{enumerate}
      This accounts for one of only \Sexpr{nrow(tokensAll[tokensAll$ProType == "1pl" & tokensAll$ProUnder == "nous",])} tokens of \lexi{nous}\footnote{
        This was considered a tokens of \lexi{nous} rather than a phonetic variant of the Creole-marked \lexi{no} because of the verbal morphology of the verb \lexi{être} matching what is said outside of Louisiana rather than what is said in Creole-marked speech.
      } for 1pl in the corpus, a pronoun so rare in the state that it is completely unattested in some corpora \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][p.~173]{rottet_language_1995}.
      In a sense, then, Dennis' use of \lexi{nous} is a shift from his already Cajun-marked subject pronouns, helping to create a foreign, distant understanding of the imagined statement from the Acadians who landed in Louisiana in 1765.
      While \lexi{nous} probably does not have a local indexicality, most subject pronouns that would also be used by non-locals the very same that Cajuns use in general.
      This double association, highlighted already in the discussion in Section \ref{sec:pronoun_discussion} of the impact of institutional French exposure, perhaps helps index Cajun-marked pronouns as indexical of formality and various forms of distance.
      
      Shifting instead towards Creole-marked pronouns, alternatively, was rare outside of imitations.
      In fact, the example in Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_lesage2} is in part an imitation, but it is an imitation of how both a Creole speaker and the participant, also capable of speaking Creole, would speak to each other.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Oliver L. (Cajun): Si t'olé parler en n****, en créole, couru-vini, mo mo capable faire ça, aussi. (1:07)\label{sent:different_speech_lesage1}
        \item[] \longtrans{Oliver L. (Cajun): If you want to speak in *****, in Creole, Couru-Vini, I'm capable of doing that, too.}
        \item Oliver L. (Cajun): Elle s'appelle Mildred, alors quand elle elle arrive, là no gain pour parler comme ça.
              Li dit, ``Oliver, comment to yé, n****?''
              Et, ``M'apè fais okay aujourd'hui.'' (2:21)\label{sent:different_speech_lesage2}
        \item[] \longtrans{Oliver L. (Cajun): Her name is Mildred, so when she arrives, then we have to talk like this.
              She says, ``Oliver, how are you, *****?''
              And, ``I'm doing okay today.''}
      \end{enumerate}
      Oliver L. fashioned himself as an educated, well-connected local.
      He consistently used Cajun-marked subject pronouns throughout the interview save for these utterances that he voluntarily produced very early on, which he appeared to do, in part, in order to further emphasize his local credentials.
      In this case, one could perhaps say that Creole-marked subject pronouns are indexing a sort of closeness contrary to the types of distance that Cajun-marked pronouns often appear to be indexing.
      This is facilitated by the fact that many Creole-marked pronouns readily distinct from those used in French outside of Louisiana, unlike the situation for Cajun-marked pronouns.
      Other than in other French-based creoles, \lexi{mo}, \lexi{no}, \lexi{li}, and \lexi{yé}, as used by Oliver L., are unattested.
      
      In the most general of terms, then, Creole-marked subject pronouns can be employed to signal localness and closeness, whereas Cajun-marked can be employed to signal distance or disagreement.
      This plays into the constructed social hierarchy between Creoles and Cajuns.
      \lexi{Cajun} is strewn about the landscape of Louisiana as the safe, acceptable category.
      In a way, Cajun has become a mainstream category.
      Its association with Whiteness undoubtedly helps it achieve that status given Louisiana's participation in the racialized social system of the broader US.
      The result is that Cajunness is more likely to spread even beyond the borders of the state, which can readily be seen in the nation-wide commodification of ``Cajun'' food \parencite[p.~33]{bienvenu_stir_2005}.
      Creole, on the other hand, is left as more of a local category.
      The marginalization of Creoles by others also makes the category more exotic, something to be dismissed by some and to be used by others to signal their deep associations with Louisiana.

    \subsection{A slur in plain sight}
      Oliver L.'s examples of Creole-marked speech above included not only subject pronouns but also a particular word that has been seen several times already during these analyses: \lexi{n****}.
      While the focus here is primarily on subject pronouns, it would perhaps be inappropriate to leave such a glaringly loaded word unaddressed.
      The complications surrounding the meaning of this word have been highlighted already in the literature \parencite[pp.~190-191]{wendte_tale_2020}.
      Some Louisianians see it as a term of endearment that can be applied to any person regardless of ethnic or racialized identity despite its status as a racist insult being recognized by most speakers of F\&C in the present study.
      The data here suggests that only Cajuns attempt to use it both ways, whereas Creoles consistently report it in the context of racism.

      Oliver L.'s statements above attempted to use the word as a term of endearment, evidenced by the fact that he placed the word in the mouth of his interlocutor when addressing Oliver L. himself, a White Cajun.
      That it was thought to be appropriate for articulating Creole-marked speech not once but twice, however, either exemplifies a latent association between such speech and Blackness, reinforces that association, or both.

      In other cases, it was used as a general label for a group of people, as in Sentence \ref{sent:nword_alice}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Alice (Cajun): Et juste avant mon école, y avait une école de Noirs.
              Tu connais, c'était pas mêlé dans ce temps-là.
              Ça se fait, moi et mes deux petits frères, on passait loin dans le fossé parce qu'on avait peur des petits N****s.
              Pourquoi on avait peur?
              Moi, je connais pas. (10:26)\label{sent:nword_alice}
        \item[] \longtrans{Alice (Cajun): And just in front of my school, there was a Black school.
              You know, it wasn't mixed at that time.
              So, my little brothers and I, we would pass far away through the trench because we were afraid of the little *****s.
              Why were we afraid?
              I don't know.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Alice used the word in question, in a rather casual, matter-of-fact way, rather than a term such as \lexi{Noirs} \gloss{Black people}, which she had already used in the same utterance.
      Judy likewise volunteered the term in a matter-of-fact way while also demonstrating a familiarity with an alternative in Sentence \ref{sent:nword_judy}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Judy (Cajun): In fact, la femme que t'as vue à Maurice, ... elle disait, tu connais elle les appelait des N****s.
              C'est ça.
              On a tous dit ça quand on était petits. ...
              Et à ce temps-ça, le monde blanc voulait pas que le monde noir vient à la maison. (51:59)\label{sent:nword_judy}
        \item[] \longtrans{Judy (Cajun): In fact, the woman that you saw in Maurice, ... she would say, you know she would call them *****s.
              That's just it.
              We all said that when we were little. ...
              And at that time, White people didn't want Black people to come to the house.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Notably, Judy, Alice, and Oliver L. are all Cajuns.
      This casual usage as an attempt at a term of endearment or as what appears to be considered by the speakers to be a neutral label for a group was limited to Cajuns in the data.

      The word wasa also involved in language ideologies, as Cynthia explained in Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_cynthia}, reproducing an extending the latter part of her utterance reported in Sentence \ref{sent:creole_meta}.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Cynthia (Cajun): Il y a des Noirs qui parlent le français comme nous-autres, mais il y a des Blancs qui parlent le créole comme les Noirs, et Cecilia qui est sept miles d'ici, pour la plupart, les Blancs, ils parlent créole, et on disait ils se parlent n****.
              Ça disait pas ils se parlent créole. (45:13)\label{sent:different_speech_cynthia}
        \item[] \longtrans{Cynthia (Cajun): There are some Black people who speak French like us, but there are some White people who speak Creole like Black people, and Cecilia which is seven miles from here, for the most part, White people, they speak Creole, and we would say to each other that they speak *****.
              They wouldn't say that they speak Creole to each other.}
      \end{enumerate}
      Cynthia explanation of how such speech was labeled is in line with Bruce's explanation in Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_bruce1} as well as encounters with White\footnote{
        This identification is a personal judgment as not every encounter in the community involved asking individuals to racially identify themselves.
      } members of the community who did not become participants in the study.
      One such individual was ashamed of his speech and did not want to say what it was called out loud but clearly was referring to the terminology Cynthia and Bruce used, and another individual who claimed to speak both French and Creole described the latter, a language variety for which he expressed a distaste, in the terminology used by Cynthia and Bruce.
      Creole-marked speech is not just associated with Blackness in these cases but is also simultaneously devalued through the use of a pejorative term.

      When Creoles used the term, in contrast, it was placed in the mouths of White people who were carrying out acts of discrimination.
      Both Gene and Tracy produced tokens of the term in Sentences \ref{sent:life_gene2} and \ref{sent:life_tracy2} where they discussed their experiences playing baseball.
      \begin{enumerate}[resume=sentences]
        \item Gene (Creole): No jouait contre un et l'autre.
            J'ai jamais joué contre les Blancs, because les Blancs étaient pas voulaient mettre pas avec les n****s. (35:54)\label{sent:life_gene2}
        \item[] \longtrans{Gene (Creole): We would play against one another.
              I never played against White people, because White people didn't want to be put with *****s.}
        \item Tracy (Creole): Mo joué un tas de la pelote, et no couru un tas les places, et mo tais seul Noir, et tous les Blancs, les autres Blancs, yé taient coutume mander, ``Comment vous-autres peut gain n**** là pour jouer avec ça?'' (95:31)\label{sent:life_tracy2}
        \item[] \longtrans{Tracy (Creole): I played a lot of baseball, and we went to a lot of places, and I would be the only Black person, and all the White people, the other White people, they had a habit of asking, ``How can you all have that ***** you play with?''}
      \end{enumerate}
      Situating these uses of the term in conflicts between White and Black people is plainly suggestive of its association with racism despite Cajuns being reticent to acknowledge that or attempting to hedge their presentations of the term.
      This speaks to not only the different social positions into which Creoles and Cajuns have been placed but also the consistently strong associations of Blackness with Creoles and Whiteness with Cajuns.

  \section{Discussion}
    The discourse of Creoles and Cajuns with respect to how they mark, police, and maintain group boundaries is congruent with the idea that these groups have been racialized.
    Where evidence of this was most lacking was in policing and maintaining boundaries (\foreign{i.e.}, denying others access to the one's group and sanctioning in-group members for accessing other groups, respectively) as the data gathering protocol, one-on-one discussions with the interviewer, simply did not provide many opportunities to carry out these tasks save for during the process of naming alters.
    The work of \textcite{myers_race_2001} suggested that the dominant group would perform more policing of boundaries and the marginalized group more maintaining.
    One result that is suggestive of this pattern is the fact that Cajuns had much to say about Creoles but Creoles had little to say about Cajuns.
    While this was not in and of itself policing and maintaining of boundaries, it did speak to the power differentials between the groups that would allow Cajuns to police boundaries and Creoles only to maintain boundaries in that Creoles may not have felt that they had the right to analyze the dominant racial group.
    They also may have had little to say due to a lack of access to Cajuns, but this was true reciprocally and did not prevent Cajuns from speaking about Creoles, so the former explanation is perhaps more plausible.

    Much more robust evidence of racialization came rather from how participants marked the boundaries of Cajunness and Creoleness in their discourse.
    In particular, there was a distinct contrast between how Cajuns were marked by Acadian ancestry whereas Creoles were marked by Blackness.
    Acadian ancestry essentially functioned as a proxy for Whiteness, allowing Cajuns to be described as White without forcing one to say the more loaded term \lexi{White}, a term that only one younger Cajun participant used frankly.
    This likewise came out in how Cajuns bent the Acadian requirement in that those with non-Acadian family names could still be Cajun if those names were at least European and especially if there was intermarriage between Europeans and Acadians in one's lineage.
    While non-Europeans (\foreign{e.g.}, Indians) may be found among the ancestors of some Cajuns, as Terry could assert as a Creole, Cajuns themselves did not confirm this either because it was not true for them or because they could ignore that part of their background as long as they pass visibly as White.
    Indeed, while exceptions could be made for qualifying alters as Cajun who did not exactly meet the condition of having pure Acadian ancestry, those exceptions never extended to those who could not pass as White.
    These discourse patterns speak to the alignment between Cajuns and Whiteness place them at the top of a racial hierarchy that excludes Creoles and Blackness.

    Creole boundaries were consistently marked, on the other hand, with reference to Blackness.
    Creoles themselves did this by speaking about mixtures and African ancestry, likely in order to dull the stigma by asserting that they are not exactly the same as Black people of the broader US, but the overall effect was that Blackness was central to marking the Creole boundary.
    While the explicit foregrounding of Blackness might presumably make it more difficult for others to cross the boundaries of Creoleness than it would the boundaries of Cajunness, the opposite was the case.
    By appealing to historical, non-racialized definitions of Creoles, those who did not pass as Black in any way could still claim a Creole identity, whereas the inverse with claiming a Cajun identity was in no way possible.
    This again reaffirms the racial hierarchy in that no privileges are gained by claiming a Creole identity given that Creoles are the marginalized group in the racialized social system.

    Participants, particularly Black Creoles, were generally forthcoming with examples of that marginalization.
    Exemplifying the use of \lexi{we} and \lexi{they} that \textcite{blanton_chronotopic_2011} found in his work that was used to distance speakers from the views found in their community, Owen used \lexi{on} \gloss{someone} to reference whoever decided that he needed to be a token during his career as an educator rather than naming the person or people involved in that decision.
    As a Black Creole, this strategy was likely born out of his need to be more careful about what he says.
    Cajuns, on the other hand, were able to ignore racial issues such as these.
    At times, this came out as a sort of obliviousness to the existence of racial marginalization among those who were in fact providing examples, such as Judy's confusion over why weaving practices were not shared between Cajuns and Creoles despite the physical closeness of the two groups.
    She described a clear example of segregation in the same breath but did not seem aware that she had her own question with respect to the lack of cultural diffusion in that area.
    Cajuns were not completely blind to racial discrimination, but they had the privilege of ignoring all but the most blatant occurrences such as \foreign{de jure} institutional segregation.

    Language was another area where Cajuns emphasized fluency in F\&C as a significant way to mark Cajun boundaries.
    In practice, however, they were much more flexible in disregarding this condition while Whiteness still remained necessary.
    Austin gave the starkest example of this by allowing his monolingual anglophone brothers to be considered Cajun simply because they are White and their ancestors would have spoken F\&C, although he was not the only Cajun who identified White anglophones as Cajun.
    Similarly, while Cajuns were clearly cognizant of the association of Creole-marked linguistic features as being used by Creoles, they also allowed those who passed as White but used Creole-marked language to be considered Cajun.
    Cynthia provided perhaps the clearest example of this in Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_cynthia} where she imitated Creole speech with the subject pronouns \lexi{mo} and \lexi{no} but explained that Cajuns in certain towns also speak that way.

    Creole-marked speech was not only associated with Creoles but with Blackness.
    Oliver L., in his demonstrations of speaking Creole used the word \lexi{nègre} each time, albeit without the intention of denigrating speakers.
    Bruce also provided a token of this term in Sentence \ref{sent:different_speech_bruce1} when he explained that someone somewhere uses the term \lexi{français nègre} for Creole speech.
    Indeed, Bruce, a Cajun, did not name any particular person in the statement but instead used an impersonal expression, much like the inverse of Owen's statement where he avoided implicating those who had wronged him in explicit terms by using the indefinite pronoun \lexi{on}.
    In this case, Bruce was distancing himself from the wrongs committed by, presumably, his own in-group members.

    There was thus a pattern of pride that was placed on using Cajun-marked F\&C and marginalization of Creole-marked F\&C.
    Further evidence of this hierarchy that runs in tandem with the racial hierarchy was the sociolinguistic labor \parencite{holliday_sociolinguistic_2021, williams_looking_2010, young_nah_2009} that Creoles felt a need to perform that Cajuns did not.
    Whereas a Cajun such as Oliver L. could use Creole-marked speech due to being raised in part by a nanny with those speech patterns, a 
    situation which itself speaks to the racial hierarchy of South Louisiana, Creoles on the other hand learned Cajun-marked speech out of the necessity to get along with Cajuns.
    This then found expression through regular code-switching, as with Owen, or mixed linguistic patterns that suggest an attempt to partially accommodate one's interlocutor, as with Gene.
    Unsurprisingly, language was not used to mark the boundaries of Creoleness.

    Other cultural products and practices were used by Cajuns and Creoles alike to mark the groups' boundaries.
    These markers provided particularly striking examples of racialization as they generally followed a pattern where a cultural product or practice was shared by both groups but the Creole version, which may in fact be identical, was treated as necessarily different or perhaps even corrupted.
    It was not always the case that culture was shared, as Judy's weaving example demonstrates, but this pattern was clearly followed with cooking and music.
    Creoles were described as making gumbo, just as Cajuns do, but with a single extra ingredient that apparently made it so different that Errol, for example, shifted from explaining that he enjoyed Creole food to saying that he did not like gumbo made in this way within the space of a single exchange.
    The same pattern occurred again with a Creole musician who plays Cajun music being described as not playing it the same in generally vague terms.
    This approach of acknowledging shared cultural products and practices while denying Creoles true ownership of those products and practices is what one would expect to see in a racialized social system.

    The differential cultural boundary marking effectively treats the public sphere as belonging to Cajuns.
    Cajun language is the default that Creoles must attain.
    Local food and music belongs fully to Cajuns and only partially to Creoles.
    This also resulted in Black Creoles, much like Black Americans in general, not always having access to institutional forms of redress when wronged, as described by Gene when given a driving ticket and Tracy when playing baseball.
    Despite this marginalization, there was no clear evidence of Creoles buying into negative markings that have been applied to them as \textcite{perkins_discursive_2019} found for Black Americans generally.
    Creoles appeared to  maintain a sense of self-worth despite the effects that the racialized social system has had on them.


  % Possibly useful thematic references
    % Social meaning
      % Myers-Scotton (1988/2000) noted that the maintenance of various languages, dialects, and styles in a community likely comes down to all languages, dialects, and styles indexing social meanings that are useful to their speakers (127)
        % Similarly, L. Milroy (1980/1987) suggested that vernacular variants may still have social significance even when usage decreases in adulthood, as she suggests that women's usage of (th) in Belfast is still affected by their social network structure, even though their usage relative to men is much lower (206)
      % A social action is "a behavior to which people give meaning," taking into account the actions and reactions of individuals (Weber, as cited in Andersen & Taylor 2013:15)
        % The use of language can perhaps be seen as a social action that triggers social meanings
      % Definitions
        % Blom & Gumperz (1972/2000) defined social meaning as the social value implied when an utterance is used in a certain context (110)
        % Meaning in general has been defined as "the conventional association of distinctions in the world with distinctions in linguistic form" (Eckert & Labov 2017:469)
        % Social meaning has been defined as "the meaning of variation" (i.e., the meaning that is evoked through varying linguistic forms being used) (Eckert & Labov 2017:469)
        % For Eckert & Labov (2017), meaning and social meaning are both socially constructed, but the latter is different in that it's purely social (469)
      % Podesva (2011) suggested that a linguistic feature must be salient in order to acquire social meaning (235)
        % For phonetic features, he measured this through the extremeness of the value of the feature and the frequency of the feature (i.e., low frequency makes it noticeable when used) (Podesva 2011:237)
      % Eckert & Labov (2017) expand Agha's (2003) enregisterment concept to refer to how any linguistic form or element comes to have a social meaning
      % Macro-social variables have been argued to only have an indirect connection with social meaning in that the variation might index local qualities (e.g. refinement, toughness, etc.) that are linked to macro-social variables (e.g. toughness > men perhaps) (Eckert & Labov 2017:469-470)
      % A decontextualized sound has no social meaning but a sound in a given context can have social meaning (Eckert & Labov 2017:481)
      % In Terrebonne-Lafourche, Rottet (1995) expected French to end up having nothing more than "a purely symbolic function" (308)
      % Eckert (2000) believed that variants of linguistic variables can index social identity ("what people are") and/or participation in particular practices ("what people do") (139)
        % This turned into her indexical field, which is a way of visualizing the different types of meaning that a variable's realizations can have through diagrams (Eckert 2008 "Variation", as cited in Bell 2014:272)
      % Bell (2014) proposed a model of how social meaning is created through language that meshes with Labov's (1972) indicators, markers, and stereotypes, Agha's (2003) enregisterment, and Silverstein's (2003) orders of indexicality (269-270)
    % Identity construction
      % All the way back to Labov (1966/2006), he was already noting that two influences on language variation are the pressure to identify with a particular group and the need to conform to the overall hierarchy of values imposed by the community (291)
      % Frameworks
        % Mead viewed identity as being developed consistently throughout one's life via learning to play roles, one's self being divided into "I" (creative unique self) and "me" (passive conforming self) (as cited in Andersen & Taylor 2013:81-84)
        % Mendoza-Denton (2001) defines social identity as "the active negotiation of an individual's relationship with larger social constructs ... signaled through language and other semiotic means" (as cited in Androutsopoulos 2008 "Style":282)
        % Bricolage is the act of forming one's identity by improvising with the cultural resources that are available (Lévi-Strauss 1966, as cited i Bucholtz 2010:11)
          % Bricolage has been argued to be useful for constructing identities when the materials used already have associations with personae that make them meaningful (Zhang 2005:457)
        % Stance is "a social actor's public display of her or his orientation to ongoing interaction with respect to both the talk at hand and to the other participants" (Du Bois 2007, as cited in Bucholtz 2010:8)
          % Bucholtz (2010) argued that "stances serve as the building blocks of identity," in the sense that the combination of indexical stances leads to the construction of identity (8-9)
        % Performativity has been defined as constructing an identity "through a 'stylized repetition of acts'" (Butler 1988, as cited in C. Cutler 2007:527)
          % Bauman (2004) describes "performance" as "a display of communicative virtuosity" (as cited in Androutsopoulos 2008 "Style":285)
        % It has been suggested that how people relate themselves in contrast to out-group members was more important in defining an ethnicity than any internal attribute of the group (Barth 1969, as cited in Irvine & Gal 2000:74-75)
        % Bucholtz (1999) proposed two linguistic mechanisms for identity construction: negative identity practices and positive identity practices (211)
        % The acts of identity model is based on the idea that linguistic behavior reveals ones attempts to show solidarity with or difference from a group (Le Page & Tabouret-Keller 1985:14/182, slightly modified from Le Page 1968)
      % Examples
        % A high Afro-centrality index for African American youths in Chapel Hill, NC did not lead to a statistically significant difference in their rate of use of AAE features, and in fact the coefficient suggested a decrease in rate as the index increased anyway (Van Hofwegen & Wolfram 2010:447)
        % In the South Carolina Sea Islands, a pair of speakers, one White and one Black, both were documented using (English-based) creole phonological features but only the Black speaker used creole morphosyntactic features (Rickford 1985, as cited in Fought 2013:390-391)
          % Fought concludes from this that these phonological features simply index island identity whereas the morphosyntactic features index Creole identity
        % German hip-hop forums, due to their "liminal" nature, provide spaces for users to "experiment with social identities and language styles in ways that are clearly outside their normal, everyday repertoire" (Androutsopoulos 2008 "Style":290)
        % 65% of the Peurto Ricans in NYC that Poplack (1979/1980/2000) interviewed felt positive about that identity, and while 90% felt that Spanish was very important, 60% still felt that being English monolingual was not a problem (218-219)
      % Examples not implicating race or ethnicity
        % Heath uses falsetto to create a flamboyant diva persona (Podesva 2007:489-490)
        % Blom & Gumperz (1972/2000) argued that Norwegians in Hemnesberget maintained two separate dialects because it allowed them to express cultural identities (109-110), specifically repeating that the local Ranamål dialect was linked to a local identity (as opposed to non-local)
          % This highlights the importance of location over ethnicity or race, something that may be in play in my data
    % Language prestige
      % Prestige has been defined as a value placed on a language variety in terms of it's usefulness specifically for social advancement (Weinreich 1953/1967:78-79)
      % Bourdieu (1977/1982) suggested that language varieties only obtain value via their distinctions from other varieties (41)
      % In Hemnesberget, Norway, Blom & Gumperz (1972/2000) found that the local variety of Norwegian, Ranamål, was more prestigious than other varieties for the residents (103) where residents would even make jokes about the way others spoke (111)
        % Ranamål is prestigious to locals despite it not being one of the two standard dialects of Norwegian that are legally sanctioned (Blom & Gumperz 1972/2000:103-104)
    % Insecurity
      % The reflexive stigma principle is the idea that the groups that use a stigmatized form the most tend to also stigmatize it the most (Labov 1966/2006:279/292)
      % Labov (1966/2006) found women in New York City to have more linguistic insecurity than men (320-322)
        % He measured this by providing a list of words and asking which pronunciation of these words is correct and which pronunciation they themselves use, the disagreements added up to form the insecurity index (95)
        % However, I would argue that he was identifying linguistic insecurity when he showed hypercorrection from the 2nd highest socioeconomic class (151-152)
          % Indeed, Labov (1966/2006) went on to say that his measure of linguistic insecurity had the 2nd highest socioeconomic group as the most insecure (320)
        % Eckert (2000) argued that the three social classes that tend to show linguistic insecurity are women, the lower middle class, and adolescents (29-30)
      % In terms of how one perceives their own speech, Labov (1966/2006) in 2006 argued based on self-reports not matching production that one often hears oneself say what one believes one should say, as opposed to using tangible feedback (313-315)
        % One example was Jewish participants in NYC who significantly over-stated their usage of prestige forms of vowels (Labov 1966/2006:312-313)
      % In Cusco, Peru, locals deny having features that are associated with rural Quechua-accented Andeans, which Delforge (2012) considers a case of erasure à la Irvine & Gal (2000) (315-316)
      % Neumann (1985) claimed that White creolophones were more ashamed of their speech than Black creolophones and so were less likely to pass it on to their children (42)
    % Power differentials
      % Symbolic privilege is a sort of privilege in relation to a symbolic system in which "[t]he privileged can assume their own positions to be norms toward which everyone else orients" (Eckert & McConnell-Ginet 1992:483)
        % Bucholtz (2010) argued that "[t]he hegemony of whiteness is typically exerted not by calling attention to white racial dominance but instead by treating whiteness as unremarkable -- even unnoticeable" (15)
          % While this provides Whites with a privileged position, it can also "leave European Americans who lack a strong ethnic identity with the sense of not 'having a culture'" and so make them inclined to appropriate features of other cultures (Frankenberg 1993; Perry 2001, as cited in Bucholtz 2010:16)
        % Minority groups tend to be the ones who need to learn the languages or majority groups but not vice versa
          % Weinreich (1949/1950) explained that, in Sutselva, Switzerland, basically all Romansh speakers are bilingual but German speakers rarely learn Romansh (as cited in Weinreich 1953/1967:84-85)
            % Romansh is a minority language in Switzerland (Berthele 2021)
          % Neumann (1985) claimed that very few were bilingual in Creole and Cajun, but those who were were also White (29)
      % In Cane Walk, Guyana, the Estate Class (i.e., fieldworkers) use Creole variants despite proficiency in non-Creole variants as a revolutionary act against the Non-Estate Class (Rickford 1986:218)
      % Eckert & McConnell-Ginet (1992) argued that gender differences in language arise out of both dominance and (cultural) difference in that these two concepts feed into each other (462)
        % This could be generalized to any social situation where there are differences
        % Eckert & McConnell-Ginet (1992) argued that dual-culture theory, in their case the difference approach to women's language, deals with power differentials in that it could predict that oppressed people are more aware of the norms of their oppressors than vice versa, but it doesn't give enough agency to actors in order for there to be any blame (467)
    % Discrimination
      % Domination is defined as control of the activities of others and exploitation as "the acquisition of economic benefits from the laboring activity of those who are dominated" (Wright 2015:9)
      % Based on speech linked to racial or ethnic identities
        % In New York, listeners were able to easily identify the race of Black and Hispanic speakers but not so much White and Asian speakers (Newman & Wu 2010:161) nor were they successful at distinguishing on a more fine-grained level between Chinese and Korean speakers (Newman & Wu 2010:157)
        % Thomas et al. (2010) cite a large list of studies showing high accuracy (70%-90%) for identifying African Americans by their speech from the 1950s up to 2000 (265-266)
          % Specifically, Thomas et la. (2010) found that vowel quality, phonation for men, and intonation for women were significant cues for helping listeners identify the speakers as African American (268)
        % Housing can be denied based on sounding Black on the telephonne in the US from places as varied as Pennsylvania, Missouri, Ohio, and California (Baugh 2015)
      % More generally
        % "[R]acialized social systems are societies that allocate differential economic, political, social, and even psychological rewards to groups along racial lines" (Bonilla-Silva 1997:474)
      % A typical form of address on German hip-hop forums is the word digga (Androutsopoulos 2008 "Style":300)
        % This moves the bar from a racist term to a reappropriated term of address within the community that is the target of said racism to further decontextualization away from its origin in German hip-hop
          % Androutsopoulos (2008 "Style") himself acknowledges this general trend of reinterpreting the African American aspects of hip-hop culture into local contexts around the world (281)
        % Speaking more general about English borrowings on German hip-hop forums, participant Wolfgang explains that he would never speak this way in person (Androutsopoulos 2008 "Potentials":15)

